WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.740
Correct Content-Type behavior matters because browsers use declared content types to decide how to handle a response.

2
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:17.160
An HTML page, JavaScript file, CSS file, JSON response, image, downloadable document, and plain text response all have different expectations.

3
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:23.420
If the content type is wrong or ambiguous, browser handling may be surprising.

4
00:00:23.700 --> 00:00:30.180
X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff tells supporting browsers not to reinterpret declared content types.

5
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:35.800
In practical terms, it helps enforce the server's declared type rather than letting the browser guess.

6
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:43.340
That is useful only when the application and hosting layers serve files and API responses with accurate content types.

7
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.620
Static files and API responses should be reviewed together.

8
00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:58.600
Static file middleware, file download endpoints, generated reports, API JSON responses, error responses, and CDN-cached assets may come from different layers.

9
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:05.180
If one layer serves inaccurate content types, a header baseline alone will not fix the underlying response quality.

10
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:12.420
Referrer-Policy helps control how much URL information the browser sends when navigating or loading resources.

11
00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:17.960
A strict policy can reduce unnecessary cross-origin sharing of full URLs.

12
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:27.260
This matters because URLs often end up in logs, analytics tools, third-party services, browser history, support screenshots, and operational systems.

13
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:30.580
Sensitive data should not appear in URLs.

14
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:42.920
Secrets, tokens, personal data, financial references, private identifiers, or sensitive workflow state can spread through logs, referrers, bookmarks, browser history, and monitoring systems.

15
00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:49.500
Referrer-Policy reduces some sharing, but it is not a substitute for keeping sensitive values out of URLs in the first place.

16
00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.060
Information exposure should be reviewed across response paths.

17
00:01:53.160 --> 00:02:05.480
MVC pages, Razor Pages, APIs, static assets, downloads, error pages, redirects, and third-party resource loads may each carry different header behavior.

18
00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.780
The baseline should reduce unnecessary exposure while preserving application functionality.
